Hi D-RUGies,

A bit late, but here's your reminder that our weekly work session is on for tomorrow at 10am, in the DSI (360 Shields <http://dsi.ucdavis.edu/directions.html>). Bring your questions/work and enjoy the space.

PACKAGE OF THE WEEK:

Currently there are 11, 597 total packages on CRAN <https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/>. This week a package I'd like to put out in the ether is riverdist <https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/riverdist/index.html>. Reads river network shape files and computes network distances. Also included are a variety of computation and graphical tools designed for fisheries telemetry research, such as minimum home range, kernel density estimation, and clustering analysis using empirical k-functions with a bootstrap envelope. Tools are also provided for editing the river networks, meaning there is no reliance on external software.

If you have a package you really enjoy using that's a hidden gem, or a fun new package you just discovered, etc., please send them my way! I'd prefer to try to focus on the packages that are a bit outside of the core/popular packages folks use (i.e., tidyverse, lattice, etc), but happy to take whatever feedback you all have. Ultimately I just want a sentence or two about the package or how you use it, a link to the vignette, and call it good. Here's a google form we can use to compile all of these:

https://goo.gl/forms/VzzeldnaEOkNImJ73

SPYvsSPY: For this week, I'll mention a recent CRAN addition that catalogues data from all the Spy vs. Spy comics (from MAD Magazine). Sort of a fun dataset for statistical examples or just playing around with. It's on CRAN, and on github here <https://github.com/shabbychef/SPYvsSPY>.

Adios,
Ryan

